libperformant_isabelle_ml -- the native half of Performant_Isabelle_ML

This directory holds the C sources behind those Isabelle/ML modules that need
native code.  There are two, each consumed by the ML file of the same name:
thread_cpu.c, the per-thread CPU clocks read by library/thread_cpu.ML, and
xxhash.c, the streaming XXH3 128-bit hash of library/xxhash.ML, on which every
persistent theory hash (library/theory_hash.ML) depends.

xxhash.h is xxHash's own single-header implementation, vendored unmodified:
  source   https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash, tag v0.8.3, file xxhash.h
  SHA-256  17973c0dc49d9854ca26caa191f0e12f7a424b68858d9a78de3860d959d85e4b
  license  BSD-2-Clause (the notice is at the head of the file)
xxhash.c includes it with XXH_INLINE_ALL, so none of its symbols is exported.

Build it once per machine: ./build with no argument lists the supported
targets; ./build TARGET compiles the library, checks it, and only then installs
it as ./TARGET/libperformant_isabelle_ml.so (on Windows
./TARGET/performant_isabelle_ml.dll).  The checks: test_xxhash, run against the
staged library on every target, Windows included (it loads the library with
dlopen or LoadLibrary, the way Poly/ML will); test_thread_cpu, run on every
target but x86_64-windows, where it cannot run (it is POSIX: dlopen, pthreads)
and a static check stands in -- the linked file is a 64-bit PE exporting
tc_self, tc_read and tc_free.  TARGET is Poly/ML's ML_PLATFORM with any "_32"
stripped from the architecture, e.g. x86_64-linux for both x86_64-linux and
x86_64_32-linux.

The built library is deliberately not committed (.gitignore covers ./*/), so a
git checkout builds it once per machine -- and again after a pull that changes
these sources, followed by a restart of Isabelle: Poly/ML keeps a loaded
library for the life of the process.  A library built before xxhash.c existed
reports an undefined symbol xxh128_create.  The conda package, by contrast,
ships it prebuilt: the `native` job of ../../.github/workflows/release-conda.yml
runs this script for each of the five targets on a GitHub-hosted runner of that
architecture at release time, and all five ./TARGET/ directories go into the one
noarch package -- library/thread_cpu.ML and library/xxhash.ML pick the directory
for the running platform, so a single artifact is correct everywhere.  Getting
them past rattler-build's source copy needs `use_gitignore: false` in
conda/recipe.yaml, since .gitignore hides exactly these directories; the recipe
asserts all five are present, so a package cannot build green without them.

A missing library, or one that will not load: Thread_CPU has no clock
(library/thread_cpu.ML, WITHOUT A CLOCK), and every persistent theory hash
raises an ERROR -- there is no fallback (library/xxhash.ML).  Both name this
build command.  The prebuilt Linux libraries require glibc 2.34 or newer
(Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, RHEL 9), where pthread_getcpuclockid moved into libc;
the macOS ones are stamped for macOS 11.0 or newer but were measured only on 14
and 15.  On an older system ./build TARGET produces a local copy with no such
requirement.

What has actually been measured for thread_cpu.c, per platform, is recorded in
../../ai-artifacts/THREAD_CPU_MEASUREMENTS.md; the header of each .c file states
the contract that follows from it.  test_thread_cpu re-measures the part of it
that a build can regress -- accuracy, a sleeping thread, a cross-thread read,
the handle after the thread has ended, and (asserted on macOS) that a held
handle is not reissued to new threads -- on every build except x86_64-windows;
Windows behaviour rests on the measurements in
../../ai-artifacts/THREAD_CPU_MEASUREMENTS.md section 4 instead.  test_xxhash
checks digests pinned from Python's xxhash.xxh128 over every XXH3 length class,
whole and in pieces, and over files (empty, larger than the 64 KiB read block, a
non-ASCII name), and that a missing file and a directory fail.
